1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,540
Deploying is the moment of truth.

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:03,962
You push a new version,

3
00:00:03,962 --> 00:00:04,519
and hope.

4
00:00:04,519 --> 00:00:05,322
If it breaks,

5
00:00:05,322 --> 00:00:06,559
every second counts,

6
00:00:06,559 --> 00:00:09,588
and a manual fix at night is how mistakes happen.

7
00:00:10,138 --> 00:00:13,558
Today you build a deploy script that does three things.

8
00:00:13,558 --> 00:00:15,174
It releases a new version.

9
00:00:15,174 --> 00:00:17,786
It checks that the new version is healthy.

10
00:00:17,786 --> 00:00:18,843
And if it is not,

11
00:00:18,843 --> 00:00:20,335
it rolls back by itself.

12
00:00:20,885 --> 00:00:25,920
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu, with a small web server running in the lab.

13
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:28,379
First,

14
00:00:28,379 --> 00:00:30,217
how the files are laid out.

15
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:32,737
Each version lives in its own folder,

16
00:00:32,737 --> 00:00:33,759
under releases.

17
00:00:33,759 --> 00:00:34,440
Type: L S,

18
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:35,053
releases.

19
00:00:35,603 --> 00:00:37,217
Three versions: v one,

20
00:00:37,217 --> 00:00:38,537
v two and v three.

21
00:00:38,537 --> 00:00:42,131
A sim link called current points to the live one.

22
00:00:42,131 --> 00:00:42,865
Type: L S,

23
00:00:42,865 --> 00:00:43,378
dash L,

24
00:00:43,378 --> 00:00:43,965
current.

25
00:00:44,515 --> 00:00:47,264
Current points to releases slash v one.

26
00:00:47,264 --> 00:00:50,717
The web server serves whatever current points to.

27
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:52,760
Ask it which version is live.

28
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:53,535
Type: curl,

29
00:00:53,535 --> 00:00:54,029
dash S,

30
00:00:54,029 --> 00:00:56,777
local host colon eight zero eight zero,

31
00:00:56,777 --> 00:00:57,763
slash version.

32
00:00:58,313 --> 00:00:59,815
Version one is live.

33
00:00:59,815 --> 00:01:02,593
Every version also has a health page.

34
00:01:02,593 --> 00:01:03,419
Type: curl,

35
00:01:03,419 --> 00:01:04,095
dash F S,

36
00:01:04,095 --> 00:01:07,023
local host colon eight zero eight zero,

37
00:01:07,023 --> 00:01:07,999
slash health.

38
00:01:08,549 --> 00:01:08,745
OK.

39
00:01:08,745 --> 00:01:09,986
The server answers,

40
00:01:09,986 --> 00:01:11,750
so this version is healthy.

41
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:14,821
Switching versions is just moving the sim link,

42
00:01:14,821 --> 00:01:15,801
which is quick,

43
00:01:15,801 --> 00:01:16,911
and easy to undo.

44
00:01:18,961 --> 00:01:22,545
Now the deploy script. Type: cat release dot S H.

45
00:01:23,095 --> 00:01:24,983
It remembers the old version,

46
00:01:24,983 --> 00:01:26,416
switches the sim link,

47
00:01:26,416 --> 00:01:28,174
then calls the health page.

48
00:01:28,174 --> 00:01:29,476
If the check passes,

49
00:01:29,476 --> 00:01:30,713
it reports success.

50
00:01:30,713 --> 00:01:31,169
If not,

51
00:01:31,169 --> 00:01:33,968
it points the link back to the old version,

52
00:01:33,968 --> 00:01:35,206
and exits with one.

53
00:01:35,206 --> 00:01:37,224
Check it with ShellCheck first.

54
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:39,373
Type: shellcheck release dot S H,

55
00:01:39,373 --> 00:01:39,633
and,

56
00:01:39,633 --> 00:01:39,893
and,

57
00:01:39,893 --> 00:01:40,610
echo clean.

58
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,746
Clean. A script you can trust, with a clear exit code that a pipeline can read.

59
00:01:47,796 --> 00:01:52,127
Now deploy version two. Type: bash release dot S H, v two.

60
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:53,340
Deployed.

61
00:01:53,340 --> 00:01:56,065
Ask the server which version is live.

62
00:01:56,065 --> 00:01:56,876
Type: curl,

63
00:01:56,876 --> 00:01:57,391
dash S,

64
00:01:57,391 --> 00:02:00,264
local host colon eight zero eight zero,

65
00:02:00,264 --> 00:02:01,295
slash version.

66
00:02:01,845 --> 00:02:06,517
Version two is live. Check where the link points. Type: read link, current.

67
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:13,147
Releases slash v two. Run the same deploy again. Type: bash release dot S H, v two.

68
00:02:13,697 --> 00:02:14,794
Deployed again,

69
00:02:14,794 --> 00:02:15,892
with no errors.

70
00:02:15,892 --> 00:02:17,720
The script is idempotent,

71
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:20,353
so a repeat or a retry does no harm.

72
00:02:22,403 --> 00:02:25,306
Now the test that matters: a bad release.

73
00:02:25,306 --> 00:02:27,643
Version three has no health page.

74
00:02:27,643 --> 00:02:28,351
Deploy it.

75
00:02:28,351 --> 00:02:30,263
Type: bash release dot S H,

76
00:02:30,263 --> 00:02:30,830
v three.

77
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:34,077
The health check failed with a four oh four,

78
00:02:34,077 --> 00:02:36,406
and the script rolled back on its own.

79
00:02:36,406 --> 00:02:37,632
Check the exit code.

80
00:02:37,632 --> 00:02:38,306
Type: echo,

81
00:02:38,306 --> 00:02:39,593
dollar question mark.

82
00:02:40,143 --> 00:02:40,425
One,

83
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:41,130
a failure,

84
00:02:41,130 --> 00:02:42,893
so a pipeline would stop,

85
00:02:42,893 --> 00:02:44,162
and alert someone.

86
00:02:44,162 --> 00:02:45,995
Which version is live now?

87
00:02:45,995 --> 00:02:46,771
Type: curl,

88
00:02:46,771 --> 00:02:47,265
dash S,

89
00:02:47,265 --> 00:02:50,015
local host colon eight zero eight zero,

90
00:02:50,015 --> 00:02:51,002
slash version.

91
00:02:51,552 --> 00:02:56,629
Version two, still serving users. Confirm the link. Type: read link, current.

92
00:02:57,179 --> 00:02:59,258
Back on releases slash v two.

93
00:02:59,258 --> 00:03:01,481
You can also roll back by hand,

94
00:03:01,481 --> 00:03:04,349
by deploying a known good older version.

95
00:03:04,349 --> 00:03:06,285
Type: bash release dot S H,

96
00:03:06,285 --> 00:03:06,715
v one.

97
00:03:07,265 --> 00:03:07,917
Deployed.

98
00:03:07,917 --> 00:03:09,222
Check the version.

99
00:03:09,222 --> 00:03:10,020
Type: curl,

100
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:10,527
dash S,

101
00:03:10,527 --> 00:03:13,354
local host colon eight zero eight zero,

102
00:03:13,354 --> 00:03:14,369
slash version.

103
00:03:14,919 --> 00:03:19,356
Version one is live. Rollback is just a deploy of an older release.

104
00:03:21,406 --> 00:03:22,729
Four myths to clear up.

105
00:03:23,279 --> 00:03:25,685
Myth one: if the deploy script finished,

106
00:03:25,685 --> 00:03:26,768
the deploy worked.

107
00:03:26,768 --> 00:03:26,948
No.

108
00:03:26,948 --> 00:03:30,617
Only a health check proves that the new version really works.

109
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:34,283
Myth two: rollback means restoring from a backup.

110
00:03:34,283 --> 00:03:34,474
No.

111
00:03:34,474 --> 00:03:36,000
With versioned releases,

112
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,781
a rollback is moving a link.

113
00:03:38,331 --> 00:03:41,787
Myth three: a deploy script only needs the happy path.

114
00:03:41,787 --> 00:03:41,979
No.

115
00:03:41,979 --> 00:03:43,451
Plan the failure first,

116
00:03:43,451 --> 00:03:44,219
and test it.

117
00:03:44,769 --> 00:03:47,709
Myth four: health checks must be complicated.

118
00:03:47,709 --> 00:03:47,905
No.

119
00:03:47,905 --> 00:03:51,041
Start with one simple request that must succeed.

120
00:03:51,591 --> 00:03:53,276
Release. Check. Roll back.

121
00:03:55,326 --> 00:03:59,891
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

122
00:04:00,441 --> 00:04:03,449
Keep every release in its own folder, with a current link.

123
00:04:03,999 --> 00:04:07,946
Switch versions by moving the link, not by editing files in place.

124
00:04:08,496 --> 00:04:12,144
Check health after every deploy, with a request that must succeed.

125
00:04:12,694 --> 00:04:14,998
Roll back automatically when the check fails.

126
00:04:15,548 --> 00:04:19,537
Exit with a non-zero code on failure, so a pipeline can react.

127
00:04:20,087 --> 00:04:23,010
Test the failure path on purpose, with a bad release.

128
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:25,181
To pass, you need all six.

129
00:04:27,231 --> 00:04:27,909
Your turn,

130
00:04:27,909 --> 00:04:30,281
in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

131
00:04:30,281 --> 00:04:32,585
Use a small local web server only,

132
00:04:32,585 --> 00:04:33,738
and nothing real.

133
00:04:34,288 --> 00:04:36,098
One: make three release folders,

134
00:04:36,098 --> 00:04:37,512
each with a version file,

135
00:04:37,512 --> 00:04:39,322
and a current link to the first.

136
00:04:39,872 --> 00:04:43,019
Two: start a web server on the current folder,

137
00:04:43,019 --> 00:04:44,250
with python three,

138
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:44,729
dash M,

139
00:04:44,729 --> 00:04:45,824
http dot server,

140
00:04:45,824 --> 00:04:48,150
and request the version with curl.

141
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:52,198
Three: write the release script, with a health check and a rollback.

142
00:04:52,748 --> 00:04:56,418
Four: deploy a good version, and prove with curl that it is live.

143
00:04:56,968 --> 00:05:01,277
Five: deploy a bad version, with no health file, and watch it roll back.

144
00:05:01,827 --> 00:05:06,478
Six: check the exit code, run ShellCheck, and stop the server when you finish.

145
00:05:07,028 --> 00:05:10,334
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

146
00:05:25,884 --> 00:05:26,759
Quick check.

147
00:05:27,309 --> 00:05:29,485
Why keep each release in its own folder?

148
00:05:30,035 --> 00:05:31,677
What proves that a deploy worked?

149
00:05:32,227 --> 00:05:34,190
What should happen when the health check fails?

150
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:37,279
How does a rollback work, with versioned releases?

151
00:05:37,829 --> 00:05:43,589
Objective covered: MC05-O09.

152
00:05:44,139 --> 00:05:46,208
